WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.360
Assume f of P times f star
of P equals u squared,

00:00:03.820 --> 00:00:07.030
where u is odd and greater
than one. For u equal to three,

00:00:07.100 --> 00:00:10.900
compare f of one with f of omega,
where omega is a primitive cube root.

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:15.570
Both values have magnitude three,
so their quotient has magnitude one.

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:20.185
As an actual norm example,
take f of P equals three i P.

00:00:20.460 --> 00:00:25.040
The quotient of three i by
three i omega is omega inverse.

00:00:25.660 --> 00:00:29.460
Dividing removes the common length
while retaining the relative angle.

00:00:30.680 --> 00:00:35.880
For general u, let I be the nonempty
set of distinct primes dividing u.

00:00:36.260 --> 00:00:40.620
Choose a primitive p-th root
zeta sub p for every prime in I.

00:00:41.140 --> 00:00:45.520
For a subset S, multiply its
chosen roots to obtain zeta sub S.

00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:48.020
The empty subset gives one.

00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:52.340
Each of these inputs has order dividing
the product of the distinct primes,

00:00:52.660 --> 00:00:57.100
hence dividing u squared. All these
evaluations are therefore allowed.

00:00:58.100 --> 00:01:01.500
Define delta of f by putting
even-sized subsets in the

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:05.140
numerator and odd-sized
subsets in the denominator.

00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:10.820
Equivalently, multiply f of zeta sub S
raised to negative one to the size of S.

00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:16.880
With two primes p and q, the even
subsets are empty and the pair p q.

00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:19.800
The odd subsets are the two singletons.

00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:24.640
The quotient is f of one
times f of zeta p zeta q,

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:28.340
divided by f of zeta p times f of zeta q.

00:01:29.240 --> 00:01:32.200
Now fix one prime, say p.

00:01:33.340 --> 00:01:37.320
Toggle its membership: add p when
absent, and remove it when present.

00:01:38.120 --> 00:01:41.920
This pairs every even subset
with exactly one odd subset,

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:44.360
and toggling again returns to the start.

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:48.020
Numerator and denominator thus
have equally many factors.

00:01:48.520 --> 00:01:52.880
Every magnitude is u. Cancel
those magnitudes along the pairs,

00:01:52.880 --> 00:01:57.040
leaving absolute value of
delta of f equal to one.

00:01:57.620 --> 00:02:01.960
Every division is legitimate.
At each allowed input, the norm

00:02:01.960 --> 00:02:06.700
identity gives the evaluated value times
its complex conjugate equal to u squared.

00:02:07.420 --> 00:02:10.780
Since u is positive, no
evaluated value is zero.

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:14.620
We are dividing actual
nonzero algebraic numbers,

00:02:14.620 --> 00:02:17.380
not cancelling potentially zero residues.

00:02:18.380 --> 00:02:22.460
We need the same conclusion
for every algebraic conjugate.

00:02:22.980 --> 00:02:26.540
A field embedding sigma
replaces the generating

00:02:26.540 --> 00:02:30.360
roots of unity by other roots
while respecting their equations.

00:02:31.180 --> 00:02:35.640
It acts on the coefficients as well as
the inputs, including i when present.

00:02:36.800 --> 00:02:39.400
Conjugating a root of
unity means inverting it,

00:02:39.460 --> 00:02:41.960
so sigma commutes with
complex conjugation.

00:02:42.420 --> 00:02:46.660
Apply sigma to each norm equation. The
rational number u squared stays fixed,

00:02:46.940 --> 00:02:49.720
so every transformed factor
still has magnitude u.

00:02:50.360 --> 00:02:55.320
The same subset pairing gives absolute
value of sigma of delta of f equal to one.

00:02:55.940 --> 00:02:59.940
Thus delta of f and all its
conjugates lie on the unit circle.

00:03:00.940 --> 00:03:03.240
This is one hypothesis
for Kronecker's theorem.

00:03:03.820 --> 00:03:07.420
We still have to prove that delta
of f is an algebraic integer.

00:03:08.100 --> 00:03:12.020
A quotient of algebraic integers does
not automatically have that property.

00:03:12.020 --> 00:03:15.520
The next steps address that
denominator obstruction.
